# Target C — C78 C75/train-width synthesis

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Audit: [target_c_train_track_crossroute_audit_2026-08-31.md](../../reports/target_c_train_track_crossroute_audit_2026-08-31.md).

The post-C76 audit found that the basic train-width theorem is not new to Target C.
Shared Theory Section 22 already proves the stronger defect-extended per-track span
bound, and Target B Section 24 upgrades it to exact hull support width.

The useful Target C contribution is the combination with C75's short-diagonal slack.
In the notation of Shared Theory Section 22,

`L_tau=sum_(f in tau) sin(phi_f)`

and

`L_tau + (sqrt(3)/2) s_F(tau) + outer-depth terms <= D`.

Summing over tracks gives a bound at least as strong as

`T D >= 2 sum_(f in F_4) area(f)`.

C75 adds

`area(f)>=sqrt(3)/2+c_rh(s_f-1)`.

Therefore the retained synthesis is

`sqrt(3) F_4 + 2 c_rh Sigma_diag
 + (sqrt(3)/2) b_F + H_infty
 <= T D`,

when the Section-22 endpoint terms are defined, with

`c_rh=(1-sqrt(3)/2)/(sqrt(2)-1)`.

The corresponding directional version follows by replacing `D` track by track with
`w_K(nu_tau)` using Target B Section 24 / Shared Theory Section 23.

## What is already known more strongly

Target B already proves:

- `T>=2 sqrt(F_4)` via the train crossing graph;
- exact support-depth identities;
- uniform hull-width saturation;
- sixfold phase coherence and grain decomposition;
- in the pure rhombic-disk branch,
  `liminf T/D >= 2.5395008183...`.

So the earlier Target C consequence `T>=(pi/2)D-O(1)` is valid but weaker and should
not be treated as the frontier.

## Current Target C use

The research target is now cross-route: combine the C66/C76 approach to the exact
straight-chain KKT face with Target B's macroscopic train-angle neutral/equality space.
The obstacle in Target B is higher-order diameter locking without quantitative KKT
weights; Target C's response-defect concentration is a possible way to supply exactly
that missing weight information.